.SHORT TERM...
Issued at 1205 AM PDT Mon Aug 10 2026
(Today and tonight)

Upper-level shortwave troughing developing off the West Coast Monday
will kickoff the cooling trend. The 1,000 foot marine layer will
likely remain steady, at least initially, with drizzle possible in
the mornings. The aforementioned troughing will result in an
increase of onshore flow with gusts up to 30 mph in the
afternoon/evening through gaps/passes and across ridgelines. This
will lead to elevated fire weather conditions across the higher
terrain coming off the heels of very hot and dry conditions. Minor
coastal flooding is expected during the highest high tide
(evening/overnight) for bayshore locations along the San Francisco
Bay, San Pablo Bay, and Monterey Bay through Thursday morning.

&&

.LONG TERM...
Issued at 1205 AM PDT Mon Aug 10 2026
(Tuesday through Sunday)

A reinforcing trough Wednesday will bring widespread below normal
temperatures and deepen the marine layer. Global ensemble clusters
are in agreement that heights and subsequently temperatures will
fall through Thursday as it digs. An increase in monsoonal/sub-
tropical moisture is bringing a low (up to 10%) chance for elevated
rain showers/thunderstorms with ingredients seemingly only marginal
as of now. Best chances look to be Wednesday through Thursday and
would likely favor the far southern and eastern parts of the area as
the conveyor belt of moisture stays between troughing to the west
and ridging to the east. While confidence is low, this would be a
high-impact scenario with gusty outflow winds and the potential for
new fire starts. It`s going to be a fair fight going into the
weekend between a reinforcing trough off the West Coast and a
rebuilding ridge across a majority of the rest of CONUS. The
official forecast has a slight warming trend into the weekend, most
noticeable across the Interior Central Coast.

